  id: W1_C
  era: "c. 2600\u20131900 BCE"
  title: Painted storage jar (replica)
  text: Painted storage jar (replica). Harappan potters threw large jars on the wheel, coated them with a red slip and painted them in black with bands, intersecting circles and leaf patterns. Jars like this stored grain, oil and water in Indus houses. This replica is based on vessels excavated at Harappa and Mohenjo-daro. Pottery is the most common find at Indus sites. Because styles changed over time, archaeologists use broken pieces of pots, called sherds, to date the layers in which they are found.
